  "articleSummary": "The article argues that agentic cyber threats arise not from a single breakthrough but from two multiplying trends: longer autonomous task execution and broader orchestration of concurrent sub-agents. Citing METR’s task-horizon research and Anthropic’s reported GTG-1002 campaign using Claude Code, it frames capability as duration × breadth, then explains how “LLMjacking”—using stolen cloud credentials to charge victims for LLM workloads—undermines cost-based access controls. Cheap, repeated attempts can produce substantial aggregate success even when individual attempts rarely work, while defenders face costly human review. Aimed at security teams skeptical of agentic threats, this is a pointed, darkly humorous threat analysis built around Terminator/Skynet imagery, swarms, and the recurring contrast between relentless operation and relentless billing.",
